CertHub.register({"id":"palo-alto-ngfw","vendor":"Palo Alto Networks","name":"Palo Alto Networks Certified Next-Generation Firewall Engineer","short":"NGFW Engineer","exam":"NGFW-Engineer","blurb":"Palo Alto's current firewall certification (successor to PCNSA/PCNSE) for admins and engineers who configure PAN-OS networking, device settings, Panorama and automation.","status":"check","statusNote":"Could not open paloaltonetworks.com from this environment on Sept 24, 2026 (blocked), so nothing here was read on an official page. Domain names come from search results summarizing the official NGFW Engineer datasheet. Weights of 38/38/24 are cited for the datasheet, but other secondary sources say 40/40/20. Check the datasheet for the current weights, question count and PAN-OS version before relying on them.","lastVerified":"2026-09-24","notices":[{"from":"2026-09-24","until":"2027-07-31","text":"PCNSA retired Jan 31, 2025 and PCNSE retired July 31, 2025. Existing holders stay certified for two years from the date they passed. This plan covers the NGFW Engineer certification that replaced them for firewall engineers. There is no one-to-one mapping."}],"examInfo":{"questions":"Not published here (multiple choice plus some drag-and-drop; confirm the count in the datasheet)","minutes":90,"pass":"Not published","extra":"90 minutes is from secondary sources; Palo Alto adds a 30-minute extension by default. Check the official datasheet."},"examSim":{"questions":75,"minutes":90},"sources":[{"label":"Palo Alto Networks Certified Next-Generation Firewall Engineer (exam page)","url":"https://www.paloaltonetworks.com/services/education/palo-alto-networks-ngfw-engineer"},{"label":"NGFW Engineer exam datasheet (PDF)","url":"https://www.paloaltonetworks.com/content/dam/pan/en_US/assets/pdf/datasheets/education/ngfw-engineer-datasheet.pdf"},{"label":"Palo Alto Networks certification overview","url":"https://www.paloaltonetworks.com/services/education/certification"}],"planWeeks":10,"hoursPerWeek":"6–8","domains":[{"id":1,"name":"PAN-OS networking configuration","w":38,"topics":["Interface types: Layer 3, Layer 2, virtual wire, tap, loopback, tunnel, VLAN and aggregate Ethernet (LACP); subinterfaces and 802.1Q tags","Security zones: zone types, one zone per interface, intrazone vs interzone default rules, User-ID enablement per zone","Zone protection profiles (flood, reconnaissance, packet-based) and interface management profiles","Routing: virtual routers vs logical routers (Advanced Routing Engine), static routes, OSPF, BGP, administrative distance defaults, ECMP","Policy-based forwarding with path monitoring; service routes; DHCP server/relay and DNS proxy","NAT on Layer 3 interfaces: source NAT (DIPP, dynamic IP, static), destination NAT, U-turn NAT, pre-NAT IP vs post-NAT zone in security rules","High availability: active/passive vs active/active, HA1/HA2/HA3 and backup links, priority and preemption, link and path monitoring, floating IPs","Site-to-site IPsec: IKE gateway, IKE and IPsec crypto profiles, tunnel interfaces, proxy IDs, tunnel monitoring","Quantum-resistant IKEv2 VPNs (post-quantum preshared keys) and GRE tunnels","GlobalProtect: portal, gateways (internal/external), authentication, connect methods (user-logon, pre-logon, on-demand), split tunneling, HIP objects and profiles, IPsec vs SSL tunnels"],"notes":["Datasheet domain 1: interfaces, zones, HA, routing, GlobalProtect, IPsec/quantum-resistant/GRE tunnels (subtopic numbers unconfirmed)"],"labs":["Take the free NGFW Engineer learning path courses on Palo Alto's Beacon site (beacon.paloaltonetworks.com) and do the interface, zone and routing configuration walk-throughs. Palo Alto has no free permanent PAN-OS VM. A VM-Series trial from a cloud marketplace still bills for compute.","Stand-in lab (not PAN-OS): in VirtualBox, build two OPNsense or pfSense VMs with a LAN, a DMZ and a WAN. Set up static routes and outbound NAT, then write down what the PAN-OS equivalent would be: zones, virtual/logical router, NAT rule and security rule with the post-NAT zone.","Stand-in lab: on the same OPNsense/pfSense VMs, build a route-based IPsec tunnel (VTI) and a CARP high-availability pair. Map each setting to PAN-OS objects: IKE gateway, IKE and IPsec crypto profiles, tunnel interface, and HA1/HA2 links."]},{"id":2,"name":"PAN-OS device setting configuration","w":38,"topics":["Administrator accounts: dynamic roles vs admin role profiles, API and CLI permissions","Server profiles (LDAP, RADIUS, TACACS+, SAML, Kerberos), authentication profiles and sequences, MFA","Authentication policy and Authentication Portal","Virtual systems: vsys creation, interfaces/zones/routers per vsys, shared gateway, inter-vsys traffic via external zones","Logging: log types, log forwarding profiles, syslog/SNMP/email/HTTP server profiles, Device > Log Settings, Strata Logging Service","Software and content updates: PAN-OS upgrade paths and base images, HA upgrade order, dynamic update schedules and thresholds","Certificate management: CAs, forward trust/untrust, SSL inbound inspection, SSL/TLS service profiles, certificate profiles, OCSP/CRL","Decryption exclusions for pinned and sensitive apps","User-ID sources (server monitoring, syslog listener, GlobalProtect, XML API), group mapping and Cloud Identity Engine","Management plane: permitted IPs, service routes, candidate vs running config, commits, partial commits, config locks and named snapshots","Web proxy (explicit and transparent) on supported PAN-OS 11.x platforms"],"notes":["Datasheet domain 2: authentication roles and profiles, virtual systems, logging, software updates, certificates, identity (subtopic numbers unconfirmed)"],"labs":["Build a free local CA with OpenSSL (`openssl req -x509 -newkey rsa:2048 ...`), issue a subordinate CA and a server certificate, and explain which one would be the forward trust, forward untrust and inbound inspection certificate on PAN-OS.","Stand-in lab: in OPNsense or pfSense, set up admin login against a free FreeRADIUS server with a local fallback account, a least-privilege admin group and remote syslog to a Linux VM. Write the matching PAN-OS objects: server profile, authentication profile, authentication sequence and admin role profile.","Using Palo Alto's public PAN-OS docs and release notes, write an upgrade runbook for an active/passive pair moving between two feature releases. Include the base image, content update, the order to suspend and upgrade each peer, and the checks to run afterwards."]},{"id":3,"name":"Integration and automation","w":24,"topics":["Panorama: device groups and hierarchy, pre-rules and post-rules, templates, template stacks, template variables and overrides","Panorama commit and push workflow; Panorama modes (Panorama, Management Only, Log Collector) and version requirements","PAN-OS XML API (keygen, config, op, commit) and REST API; API-only admin role profiles","Infrastructure as code: Terraform panos provider, Ansible paloaltonetworks.panos collection, pan-os-python SDK","External dynamic lists (IP, domain, URL) and dynamic address groups with tags","Auto-tagging from log forwarding profiles and HTTP server profiles for webhooks and ticketing","VM-Series bootstrapping (init-cfg.txt, bootstrap.xml, content/license/software/plugins folders) and Zero Touch Provisioning","Form factors: PA-Series, VM-Series, CN-Series, Cloud NGFW for AWS and Azure","Strata Cloud Manager and cloud-delivered management"],"notes":["Datasheet domain 3: Panorama, APIs, automation tools, bootstrapping and cloud/container deployments (subtopic numbers unconfirmed)"],"labs":["Read the free PAN-OS XML and REST API docs, then write a Python script with `requests` that builds keygen, add-address-object and commit calls against a placeholder host. Validate the URLs and XML payloads offline against the documented format, since there is no free PAN-OS VM to call.","Install Terraform (free), write a config for two address objects, an address group and a security rule with the `PaloAltoNetworks/panos` provider, and run `terraform init` and `terraform validate` (no firewall needed).","Host a text file of IPs on a local `python3 -m http.server`, import it as a URL alias in OPNsense or pfSense as a stand-in for an EDL, block it in a rule, then edit the file and watch the list refresh without a rule change."]}],"study":{"1":[["Compare Layer 3, Layer 2, virtual wire and tap interfaces: when would you choose each?","Layer 3 routes between subnets and supports NAT, VPN and routing protocols. Layer 2 switches within a VLAN. Virtual wire drops in transparently between two devices with no addressing. Tap passively monitors a SPAN copy and cannot block."],["Describe the HA links and what each one carries.","HA1: control (hellos, heartbeats, state, config sync). HA2: data (session, forwarding and IPsec SA sync). HA3: active/active packet forwarding to the session owner. Backup links protect HA1 and HA2."],["What do you need for a route-based site-to-site IPsec VPN on PAN-OS?","Tunnel interface in a zone, IKE crypto profile (phase 1), IKE gateway, IPsec crypto profile (phase 2), IPsec tunnel binding them, a route to the remote networks via the tunnel, and security rules. Add proxy IDs for policy-based peers."],["Explain the roles of the GlobalProtect portal, gateway and HIP.","The portal authenticates and delivers app config and the gateway list. Gateways terminate IPsec/SSL tunnels and enforce policy. HIP reports endpoint posture, matched in rules via HIP profiles."],["Why do security rules use the pre-NAT IP but the post-NAT zone?","The firewall evaluates NAT first to find the egress (post-NAT) zone, but the packet still carries the original addresses when security policy is checked. So inbound DNAT rules use the public IP and the DMZ zone."]],"2":[["Dynamic roles vs admin role profiles: what is the difference?","Dynamic roles are built in (superuser, device admin, vsys admin and their read-only forms) and update with new features. Admin role profiles are custom and can enable or disable individual UI tabs, CLI and API access."],["How do server profiles, authentication profiles and authentication sequences fit together?","A server profile says how to reach RADIUS, TACACS+, LDAP, Kerberos or SAML. An authentication profile uses one method and server profile plus settings like MFA. A sequence tries several authentication profiles in order."],["Walk through a safe PAN-OS upgrade on an HA pair.","Back up config, check the upgrade path and release notes, download the base and target images, update content, suspend the passive peer and upgrade it, fail over, then upgrade the other peer and verify sync."],["What certificates does decryption use, and why two for forward proxy?","Forward trust signs impersonated certs for valid sites; forward untrust signs them when the real cert is invalid so users still get a warning. Inbound inspection uses the real server's certificate and key."],["List four User-ID sources and when to use each.","Server monitoring of DC logs for Windows domains; syslog listener for NAC or wireless controllers; GlobalProtect for remote users; Authentication Portal for unknown users; XML API for custom systems."]],"3":[["Device groups vs templates vs template stacks in Panorama?","Device groups hold policies and objects (with pre-rules and post-rules). Templates hold network and device settings. Template stacks layer several templates, with the top template winning conflicts."],["How do dynamic address groups and auto-tagging work together?","A dynamic address group matches tags. Tags are registered via XML API, VM information sources or log forwarding auto-tag actions, so group membership (and rule effect) changes without a commit."],["What goes in a VM-Series bootstrap package?","Folders config (init-cfg.txt and optional bootstrap.xml), content, license (auth codes), software and plugins, stored on a cloud bucket, file share, ISO or USB."],["When would you choose Terraform vs Ansible vs raw API for PAN-OS automation?","Terraform for declarative, stateful infrastructure as code; Ansible for procedural, idempotent playbooks; raw XML/REST API or pan-os-python for custom scripts and integrations."],["Compare VM-Series, CN-Series and Cloud NGFW.","VM-Series is a self-managed virtual firewall on hypervisors or cloud instances. CN-Series runs as containers in Kubernetes. Cloud NGFW is a managed cloud-native service on AWS or Azure."]]},"qCount":149,"hasLessons":true,"hasLessonsEs":true,"hasPbqs":true,"hasLevels":true});
